tests.user_message:
Validate this Normalizer for serial classification history. Input is sorted by serial and strictly increasing at. First observation emits OPEN; a changed classification emits CLOSE with the old class/key, then OPEN with the new ones; unchanged emits nothing. at is the command effective time on both rows; a downstream applier looks up the old version start by sk. event_sk is provided upstream, null classification differs from empty. Check whether two transform calls for a change can accidentally advance carried state twice.

Input 0: serial string non-null, classification string nullable, event_sk long non-null, at date non-null. Output 0: serial string non-null, op string non-null, classification string nullable, sk long non-null, at date non-null. 

```ctl
//#CTL2
boolean hasState = false;
string activeSerial;
string activeClass;
long activeSk;
string[] actions = [];
string[] classes = [];
long[] keys = [];
function integer count() {
    clear(actions); clear(classes); clear(keys);
    boolean known = hasState && activeSerial == $in.0.serial;
    if (known && activeClass == $in.0.classification) return 0;
    if (known) {
        append(actions, "CLOSE"); append(classes, activeClass); append(keys, activeSk);
    }
    append(actions, "OPEN"); append(classes, $in.0.classification); append(keys, $in.0.event_sk);
    activeSerial = $in.0.serial;
    activeClass = $in.0.classification;
    activeSk = $in.0.event_sk;
    hasState = true;
    return length(actions);
}
function integer transform(integer idx) {
    $out.0.serial = $in.0.serial;
    $out.0.op = actions[idx];
    $out.0.classification = classes[idx];
    $out.0.sk = keys[idx];
    $out.0.at = $in.0.at;
    return ALL;
}
```

